695 0 obj
<<
/A 3066 0 R
/Alt (The four-panel diagram, arranged in one row, illustrates four types of memory and their characteristics. Panel A, titled Simple dialogue memory, shows two stacked speech bubbles. The top bubble reads &#x00022;I see four POIs nearby&#x00022;, and the bottom bubble reads &#x00022;I move to the nearest POI&#x00022;. Below this, the Memory characteristic section lists &#x00022;Unstructured conversational context&#x00022; and &#x00022;Observations and routes are stored as text tokens&#x00022;. Panel B, titled Node-sequence memory, displays &#x00022;Local observation&#x00022; above two small grid-like images. Below these are a blue circle labelled POI, a yellow pentagon labelled Int, and another blue circle labelled POI, connected by a dashed line labelled &#x00022;Route taken&#x00022;. The Memory characteristic section lists &#x00022;Chronological sequence of visited nodes&#x00022; and &#x00022;Stores local observation at each node&#x00022;. Panel C, titled Graph memory, presents a network graph with multiple interconnected circular nodes of varying sizes and solid and dashed lines representing edges. The Memory characteristic section lists &#x00022;Nodes: POIs and intersections&#x00022;, &#x00022;Edges: road connectivity&#x00022;, and &#x00022;Incrementally constructed topological graph&#x00022;. Panel D, titled Map memory, shows a grid-like map with grey roads, purple building shapes, four yellow pentagon markers, and one blue circular marker. Text labels &#x00022;Intsection&#x00022; and &#x00022;Caf&#x000E9;&#x00022; are visible near markers.)
/K 13
/P 52 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/S /Figure
/Type /StructElem
>>
endobj
